response:
 Based on your instructions and the given input array, here's how you can create a new array:

First, we will sort the array based on the `position` property in ascending order to ensure that items with smaller positions come before those with larger positions when `randomize` is set to false.

Next, we will process each item in the original array and add it to the new array either at its correct position (when `randomize` is false) or in a random position (when `randomize` is true). We will keep track of the current index in the new array and use it to determine the random position for items with `randomize` set to true.

Here's some sample code in JavaScript to achieve this:

```javascript
const inputArray = [
  { name: "apple", position: 1, randomize: false },
  { name: "pineapple", position: 2, randomize: false },
  { name: "orange", position: 3, randomize: true },
  { name: "banana", position: 4, randomize: true },
  { name: "grapes", position: 8, randomize: false },
  { name: "lemon", position: 11, randomize: true },
  { name: "jack fruit", position: 10, randomize: false },
  { name: "dragon fruit", position: 5, randomize: false },
  { name: "green apple", position: 9, randomize: false }
];

// Sort inputArray based on position property
inputArray.sort((a, b) => a.position - b.position);

// Initialize empty output array
let outputArray = [];

// Process each item in inputArray and add to outputArray
for (const item of inputArray) {
  let position;

  // Determine position based on randomize flag
  if (!item.randomize) {
    position = item.position - 1;
  } else {
    // When randomize is true, place item in a random position
    const minIndex = 0;
    const maxIndex = outputArray.length;
    position = Math.floor(Math.random() * (maxIndex - minIndex)) + minIndex;

