 In Rust, you can define the `is_even` function using a loop instead of multiple `if-else` statements. Here's how you can do it:

```rust
fn is_even(n: i32) -> bool {
 if n < 0 {
 return false; // negative numbers are odd
 }
 for _ in 0..=1 {
 if n % 2 != 0 {
 return false;
 }
 n -= 1;
 }
 true
}
```

This implementation checks if the number is even by subtracting 1 repeatedly and checking if it's divisible by 2 until it reaches 0. If it was odd at any point during this process, then the original number was odd as well.

