 I'm here to help you write a React utility function to handle the removal of widows in text. Widows refer to the last line of a paragraph that is separated from the rest of the text when a page is broken at the end of a line, leaving a single word or just a few letters on the new line. Eliminating widows can improve the overall look and readability of your text.

Here's an example of a simple utility function using the `react-break-word` library for handling line breaks:

1. First, install the library by running this command in your terminal:

```bash
npm install react-break-word
```

2. Now create a new file called "widowRemoval.js" and add the following code:

```javascript
import React, { useRef } from 'react';
import { useBreakWords } from 'react-break-word';

const WidowRemover = ({ text }) => {
  const textRef = useRef(null);
  const lines = useBreakWords(text, { maxWidth: 750 }); // Set max width as needed

  return (
    <p ref={textRef}>
      {lines.map((line, index) => (
        <span key={index}>{line}</span>
      ))}
    </p>
  );
};

export default WidowRemover;
```

3. To use this utility component in your project, import it and wrap any paragraph containing text that needs to be checked for widows:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import WidowRemover from './widowRemoval';

const App = () => {
  const text = "Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Duis ut turpis eget mauris lacinia commodo. Praesent dapibus, nulla id bibendum venenatis, augue metus volutpat nisi, vel aliquam quam nisl et tellus.";

  return (
    <div className="App">
      <WidowRemover text={text} />
    </div>
  );
};

export default App

